        <p>Here's a secret the wine world doesn't advertise: the $20 shelf is where the fun is. Nobody's paying for a famous label or a fancy box, so the wine has to earn it in the glass. These are the styles and producers we reach for ourselves, sorted by what you're actually in the mood for. No exact prices here because they shift with vintages and vendors, but everything below lands under $20 at our stores.</p>

        <h2>Reds Under $20</h2>
        <h3>Spanish Garnacha</h3>
        <p>Spain's Garnacha (the same grape as Grenache) is the best value in red wine, full stop. Look for bottles from Campo de Borja, Calatayud, or Cariñena: juicy red fruit, a little spice, soft tannins, and enough body to stand up to smoked meat. Producers like Bodegas Borsao and Las Rocas are widely available and reliable year after year.</p>
        <h3>Côtes du Rhône</h3>
        <p>The everyday red of southern France, usually a blend of Grenache and Syrah. Peppery, plummy, and food-friendly in a way that few wines at this level manage. Names to know: E. Guigal, Famille Perrin, and Domaine de la Janasse's entry-level bottling when we can get it.</p>
        <h3>Italian Montepulciano d'Abruzzo</h3>
        <p>Dark, plush, and easygoing. Montepulciano is the bottle we hand people who say they like Cabernet but don't want to spend Cabernet money. Masciarelli and Umani Ronchi are two dependable labels.</p>
        <h3>Argentine Malbec</h3>
        <p>Mendoza Malbec is built for a Texas grill: ripe blackberry, a touch of cocoa, and a smooth finish. Catena's Alamos line and Trapiche are the classics, and they're everywhere for good reason.</p>

        <h2>Whites Under $20</h2>
        <h3>Portuguese Vinho Verde</h3>
        <p>Light, lively, faintly fizzy, and usually low in alcohol, which matters in August. Vinho Verde is the porch wine of the Austin summer. Casal Garcia and Aveleda are the two you'll see most, and both are exactly what you want on ice at 4pm.</p>
        <h3>Chilean Sauvignon Blanc</h3>
        <p>Chile's coastal valleys (Casablanca, Leyda) turn out crisp, citrusy Sauvignon Blanc with a grassy edge, at a fraction of what New Zealand charges. Try Casillero del Diablo or Montes.</p>
        <h3>Spanish Albariño</h3>
        <p>From Rías Baixas on Spain's Atlantic coast: peach, salt air, and a zip of acidity. Martín Códax is the well-known name, and it's a killer match for ceviche or grilled shrimp.</p>

        <h2>Rosé Under $20</h2>
        <p>Provence gets the headlines, but the $20 shelf is deeper than that:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Southern French rosé</strong> from the Languedoc or Côtes de Provence's entry-level bottlings (Gérard Bertrand's Côte des Roses is the crowd-pleaser).</li>
          <li><strong>Spanish rosado</strong> made from Garnacha or Tempranillo: a little fuller and fruitier, great with tacos al pastor.</li>
          <li><strong>Portuguese rosé</strong> from the Vinho Verde region, slightly spritzy and made for pool days.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Bubbles Under $20</h2>
        <h3>Spanish Cava</h3>
        <p>Cava is made the same way as Champagne (second fermentation in the bottle) at a fraction of the price. Freixenet and Segura Viudas are the workhorses; Juvé &amp; Camps is the step up that still sneaks under $20 on a good day.</p>
        <h3>French Crémant</h3>
        <p>Crémant is French sparkling wine from outside Champagne, traditional method, and it's the bottle we recommend most for anyone who wants that toasty, elegant style without the Champagne tax. Crémant de Bourgogne, de Loire, and d'Alsace are all fair game.</p>
        <h3>Italian Prosecco</h3>
        <p>Softer and fruitier than Cava or Crémant, and the right call for mimosas and spritzes. La Marca and Mionetto are the reliable ones.</p>

