        <h2>Texas Wines Under $20</h2>
        <p>Texas Hill Country wine has come a long way, and several established wineries make bottles that land under $20 while still tasting like the place they're from. Look for:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Fall Creek Vineyards (Tow)</strong> &mdash; one of the state's original wineries; their entry-level reds and whites are consistent and easy to like.</li>
          <li><strong>Becker Vineyards (Stonewall)</strong> &mdash; the Iconoclast line is the budget-friendly way into Becker's Cabernet and Chardonnay.</li>
          <li><strong>Llano Estacado (Lubbock)</strong> &mdash; the big High Plains producer; their Signature and Cellar Reserve tiers are solid everyday bottles.</li>
          <li><strong>Pedernales Cellars (Stonewall)</strong> and <strong>William Chris Vineyards (Hye)</strong> &mdash; most of their lineups sit above $20, but keep an eye out for their entry blends; they're worth the stretch when they show up.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Texas does especially well with warm-climate grapes like Tempranillo, Mourvèdre, and Viognier. If you like the Spanish and Rhône bottles above, you'll like these. We stay just as local on the spirits side; see our <a href="/guides/texas-spirits-guide.html">Texas spirits guide</a>.</p>

